{"title":"1995 AHSME Problems/Problem 30","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1995_AHSME_Problems/Problem_30&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A large cube is formed by stacking 27 unit cubes. A plane is perpendicular to one of the internal diagonals of the large cube and bisects that diagonal. The number of unit cubes that the plane intersects is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/b/03bd2adceb4900561812cfec2439207820952d1f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathrm{(A) \\ 16 } \\qquad \\mathrm{(B) \\ 17 } \\qquad \\mathrm{(C) \\ 18 } \\qquad \\mathrm{(D) \\ 19 } \\qquad \\mathrm{(E) \\ 20 }$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"387\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1995_AHSME_Problems/Problem_30&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Place one corner of the cube at the origin of the coordinate system so that its sides are parallel to the axes.\n</p><p>Now consider the diagonal from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/9/4e9f36b13a11a14accae8e072c0cf34d69267bb6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"59\" height=\"20\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/5/5f5982cde213cdab41b3311daba85ba58046451e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3,3,3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" />. The midpoint of this diagonal is at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/3/733172a71d3bc12049cc120a04fa1148a5236517.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\frac 32,\\frac 32,\\frac 32\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"78\" height=\"43\" />. The plane that passes through this point and is orthogonal to the diagonal has the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/3/0c38ed5c1bbb25188755a8177761758b751b31c7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y+z=\\frac 92$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"109\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p>The unit cube with opposite corners at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/3/c73ed093cc73a8d9a14185fa525e7067e5c3725d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y,z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"58\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/a/35a20de04afad063d700203d942bc1388dd0eb09.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x+1,y+1,z+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"152\" height=\"18\" /> is intersected by this plane if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/4/31406951fe79c51d0f539d8dde6045a0867829b4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y+z &lt; \\frac 92 &lt; (x+1)+(y+1)+(z+1)=(x+y+z)+3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"488\" height=\"37\" />. Therefore the cube is intersected by this plane if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/2/a32af1210f28a9fde0d57ccdb6e5c381979e2f32.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y+z\\in\\{2,3,4\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"159\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>There are six cubes such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/a/24ab14221b6b14abd811a7d87bbe72241a34eadf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y+z=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"106\" height=\"15\" />: permutations of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/2/992be26a8d2b207f24cef4a13386cda421e070d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,1,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/b/75bac664226c205efc73bd02d9490d828f05a5b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2,0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" />. <br />\nSymmetrically, there are six cubes such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/1/5f1391cc9cd08362d372ac8c4115094d4888ca35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y+z=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"107\" height=\"15\" />. <br />\nFinally, there are seven cubes such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/5/ad5ff13ebc979a03c144ba96c88e6281c5556894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y+z=3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"109\" height=\"17\" />: permutations of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/3/d63ed178af6bfe79a068e40734a2a6a6a818f37f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2,1,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" /> and the central cube <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/d/35d43e6e252922c8df825114d48e1418c7516d6d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,1,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>That gives a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/6/3e6b4391f25789ac9419bc73abb91174108ef344.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{19}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"30\" height=\"23\" /> intersected cubes.\n</p><p>Note that there are only 8 cubes that are not intersected by our plane: 4 in each of the two opposite corners that were connected by the original diagonal.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1995_AHSME_Problems/Problem_30&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Place the cube so that its space diagonal is perpendicular to the ground. The space diagonal has length of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/7/4f7e99b6a177dd82046ee2ae91b50c143a72b417.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"35\" height=\"21\" />, the altitude of the top vertex of the newly placed cube is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/7/4f7e99b6a177dd82046ee2ae91b50c143a72b417.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"35\" height=\"21\" />. The plane perpendicular and bisecting the space diagonal is now parallel to the ground and also bisecting the space diagonal into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/1/1b1cd991e4fe587a918dff181a824ed877790235.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"35\" height=\"41\" />, so that the height of the plane is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/1/1b1cd991e4fe587a918dff181a824ed877790235.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"35\" height=\"41\" />.\n</p><p>By symmetry, the space diagonal is trisected by the pyramid at the top of the cube and the pyramid at the bottom of the cube.\n</p><p>We can prove that the space diagonal is trisected. Let the altitude of the pyramid at the top of the cube be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/8/8189a5b5a0917b8c93350827be4038af1839139d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$h$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />. The base of the pyramid is an equilateral triangle with side length of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/3/df3aabbd3e6b4f66dfbeda208a8903fce47c6f88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{3^2+3^2}=3\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"130\" height=\"21\" />. The height of the triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/9/7191736fa698b83df26d3ee9eb8bb7181a21a4f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{ \\sqrt{3} }{2} \\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"77\" height=\"43\" />. The distance of the center of the triangle to the vertex is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/9/ab9cb0fd673a5ac072f4ab62e18bf80be0e085e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac23 \\cdot \\frac{ \\sqrt{3} }{2} \\cdot 3\\sqrt{2} = \\sqrt {6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"151\" height=\"43\" />. Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/8/9c870d48e7043f59fcfeb02ec493824b397ac6f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$h = \\sqrt{3^2-(\\sqrt {6})^2} = \\sqrt{9-6}=\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -9px\" width=\"267\" height=\"35\" />. The altitude of the pyramid at the bottom of the cube is also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/8/8189a5b5a0917b8c93350827be4038af1839139d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$h$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />. The altitude in the middle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/6/3c6079a0042b0dddca1996889ec0d02ec0f3429f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{3}=\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"177\" height=\"21\" />.\n</p><p>The altitude of the vertex at the top is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/7/4f7e99b6a177dd82046ee2ae91b50c143a72b417.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"35\" height=\"21\" />. The altitude of the second highest <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> vertices are all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/5/e455d80f4657b1112ba302a6e2c42e9c81f780ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" />. The altitude of the third highest <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> vertices are all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/7/6c77cf4b63af80bfbc6a9ad9b4b9e8e549e77d78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"18\" />. The altitude of the bottom-most vertex is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>By scale, for the unit cube, place the cube so that its space diagonal is perpendicular to the ground. The altitude of the vertex at the top is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/7/6c77cf4b63af80bfbc6a9ad9b4b9e8e549e77d78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"18\" />. The altitude of the second highest <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> vertices are all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/6/1f6f572d974137fa33e72957f374faaa8f58ea32.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"35\" height=\"41\" />. The altitude of the third highest <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> vertices are all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/c/e5cb6e7c42d6bf0c332b448f0d28fff6c0aaaef1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"26\" height=\"41\" />. The altitude of the bottom-most vertex is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>The length of the space diagonal of a unit cube is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/7/6c77cf4b63af80bfbc6a9ad9b4b9e8e549e77d78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"18\" />. The highest vertex of the bottom-most unit cube has an altitude of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/7/6c77cf4b63af80bfbc6a9ad9b4b9e8e549e77d78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"18\" />. As <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/0/ed0474737eb5140e96974d3568eb0eb5d9e6e2c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{3} &lt; \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"88\" height=\"43\" />, therefore, the plane will not pass through the unit cube at the bottom.\n</p><p>For the next <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cubes from the bottom, the altitude of their highest vertex is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/3/1a3dbc43bc0a405592c671d399771ef3caa48536.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} + \\sqrt{3} = \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"138\" height=\"43\" />. As <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/5/ad51b5ff758b0fea67d74b5ee4c2a73afcc022ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3} &lt; \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"101\" height=\"43\" />, therefore, the plane will not pass through the next <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> unit cubes.\n</p><p>For the next <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cubes from the bottom, the altitude of their highest vertex is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/9/1499cfcfdd43991572dc56b5ac50997100b404d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"151\" height=\"43\" />. As <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/2/912dfbea463b677e9a11e342ec497932d645863b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{3} &gt; \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"101\" height=\"43\" />, therefore, the plane will pass through the next <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> unit cubes.\n</p><p>So at the bottom half of the cube, there are only <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/a/cca0e40dbe6b9d8507944ac92eb83b4fa405d4e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+3 = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" /> unit cubes that the plane does not passes through. By symmetry, the plane will not pass through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> unit cubes at the top half of the cube.\n</p><p>Thus, the plane does not pass through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/337cd63431c75e66027a39575c23f72e2c70b947.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4+4 = 8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" /> unit cubes, it passes through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/c/c1cc9eb097519fb1d5aa9673be9f30c21d8dc8a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$27-8=\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 19}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"141\" height=\"30\" /> unit cubes.\n</p><p>~<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/User:Isabelchen\">isabelchen</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1995_AHSME_Problems/Problem_30&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1995_AHSME\" title=\"1995 AHSME\">1995 AHSME</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1995_AHSME_Problems\" title=\"1995 AHSME Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1995_AHSME_Answer_Key\" title=\"1995 AHSME Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=44&amp;year=1995\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1995_AHSME_Problems/Problem_29\" title=\"1995 AHSME Problems/Problem 29\">Problem 29</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"cente